# The Heston model

[Back to Heston and Bates](../finance_03_heston_and_bates.md)

In the Black-Scholes model the volatility is a constant, which no option market supports: implied volatilities vary with strike and maturity. The Heston model lets the variance *v* of the asset move randomly:

dS = (r − q) S dt + √v S dW₁, dv = κ (θ − v) dt + σ √v dW₂, with corr(dW₁, dW₂) = ρ.

The variance reverts to its long-run level θ at speed κ, σ is the volatility of the variance, ρ the correlation between the asset and its variance (negative for equities: the variance rises when the price falls), and v₀ the starting variance. The characteristic function of ln S_T is known in closed form, so a European option price is a one-dimensional integral, which the Fourier engines evaluate quickly.

```cpp
instrument::Vanilla instrument::make(pricing::OptionType type, double strike, double maturity, Config cfg = f64_default());
market::State market::make(double spot, double rate, double dividend_yield, Config cfg = f64_default());
module::Model model::make(model::Kind kind, std::initializer_list<double> params, Config cfg = f64_default());
module::Field::Result module::Field::price(Config cfg = {}) const;
module::Engine module::Controls::engine = module::Engine::Analytic;
```

The model is built from its five parameters, in the order `kappa, theta, sigma, rho, v0`, and priced on the COS engine:

```cpp
mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(prc::Call, strike, maturity),
                 .market = fin::market::make(spot, rate, dividend_yield),
                 .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {kappa, theta, sigma, rho, v0}),
                 .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Cos}};
```

The model is the only change from the Black-Scholes field of chapter 1; the engine changes too, because Heston has no closed form. Four engines price a Heston field: `Cos` and `Fft` (this chapter), `MonteCarlo` (the next) and `Pde`. `fin` is `pluss::subsp::math::finance`, `mod` is `fin::module` and `prc` is `fin::pricing`. The parameters `kappa`, `theta` and `sigma` must be positive, `v0` non-negative, and `rho` must lie strictly between −1 and 1. The Feller condition 2κθ ≥ σ², which keeps the variance away from zero, is not required: the pricing formula is valid without it.

The programs of this chapter share a small helper, `strip`, that builds a field for a list of strikes and maturities under the model and engine it is given.

## Checks

Three properties pin the price down. Put-call parity holds exactly, as for any model. As σ → 0 with v₀ = θ the variance is constant and equal to θ, so the price must approach the Black-Scholes price at volatility √θ. And the price depends on the correlation: with ρ < 0 a call struck above the money is cheaper, and one struck below it dearer, than with ρ = 0.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
namespace prc = fin::pricing;
using namespace pluss::tils;
using pluss::Series;

constexpr double kSpot = 100.0, kRate = 0.03, kDiv = 0.01;

// A strip of options on one underlying priced under Heston (or Bates, with eight parameters) by the chosen engine.
mod::Field strip(mod::Engine engine, const std::vector<double> &strikes, const std::vector<double> &maturities, std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04},
                 prc::OptionType type = prc::Call, fin::model::Kind kind = fin::model::Kind::Heston) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = {.type = type, .strike = pluss::make_series(strikes), .maturity = pluss::make_series(maturities)},
                    .market = fin::market::make(kSpot, kRate, kDiv),
                    .model = fin::model::make(kind, params),
                    .controls = {.engine = engine, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
}
std::vector<double> prices(const mod::Field &f) { return f.price().price.items<double>(); }
double price1(const mod::Field &f) { return prices(f)[0]; }

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln("strike   call       put        call - put   S e^-qT - K e^-rT");
  const std::vector<double> strikes{80.0, 100.0, 120.0};
  const auto calls = prices(strip(mod::Engine::Cos, strikes, {1.0}));
  const auto puts = prices(strip(mod::Engine::Cos, strikes, {1.0}, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}, prc::Put));
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < strikes.size(); ++i) {
    out.writeln(strikes[i], calls[i], puts[i], calls[i] - puts[i], kSpot * std::exp(-kDiv) - strikes[i] * std::exp(-kRate));
  }

  // The Black-Scholes field at volatility 0.2 is the limit as the volatility of variance vanishes.
  mod::Field black_scholes{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(prc::Call, 100.0, 1.0), .market = fin::market::make(kSpot, kRate, kDiv),
                           .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::BlackScholes, {0.2}), .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
  out.writeln("vol of vol sigma -> 0 (v0 = theta = 0.04), against Black-Scholes at volatility 0.2:");
  for (double sigma : {0.3, 0.1, 0.01, 0.001}) {
    out.writeln("  sigma", sigma, " Heston", price1(strip(mod::Engine::Cos, {100.0}, {1.0}, {1.5, 0.04, sigma, -0.7, 0.04})), " Black-Scholes", price1(black_scholes));
  }

  out.writeln("correlation rho, calls struck at 80, 100, 120:");
  for (double rho : {-0.7, 0.0, 0.7}) {
    out.writeln("  rho", rho, prices(strip(mod::Engine::Cos, strikes, {1.0}, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, rho, 0.04})));
  }
}
```

Parity holds to the digits shown at each strike (21.3693, 1.9604 and −17.4485). As σ falls, the Heston price approaches the Black-Scholes price at volatility 0.2: 8.5423 at σ = 0.3, 8.7947 at 0.1, 8.826992 at 0.01 and 8.827318 at 0.001, against 8.827321. The correlation reshapes the price across strikes: with ρ = −0.7 the call at strike 120 is worth 1.477 against 2.460 at ρ = 0 and 3.234 at ρ = 0.7, and the call at strike 80 is worth more (22.83, 22.37, 21.76), which is the skew.

## The two forms of the characteristic function

The Heston characteristic function contains a complex square root and logarithm. In the original formulation of Heston (1993) the logarithm is evaluated on its principal branch, which jumps as the frequency or the maturity grows, so the integrand is discontinuous and the price is wrong for long maturities. The reformulation `HestonCfMode::LittleTrap` (Albrecher et al.) is algebraically equal but continuous, and is the default of the FFT engine (`controls.fft.mode`). `HestonCfMode::Original` is kept so that results computed with the original formulation can be reproduced; at the longest maturities the FFT engine refuses it rather than return a wrong price. The program prices a call from three months to fifteen years with the FFT engine in both forms, and with the COS engine (which uses the stable form) for reference.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
namespace prc = fin::pricing;
using namespace pluss::tils;
using pluss::Series;

constexpr double kSpot = 100.0, kRate = 0.03, kDiv = 0.01;

// A strip of options on one underlying priced under Heston (or Bates, with eight parameters) by the chosen engine.
mod::Field strip(mod::Engine engine, const std::vector<double> &strikes, const std::vector<double> &maturities, std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04},
                 prc::OptionType type = prc::Call, fin::model::Kind kind = fin::model::Kind::Heston) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = {.type = type, .strike = pluss::make_series(strikes), .maturity = pluss::make_series(maturities)},
                    .market = fin::market::make(kSpot, kRate, kDiv),
                    .model = fin::model::make(kind, params),
                    .controls = {.engine = engine, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
}
std::vector<double> prices(const mod::Field &f) { return f.price().price.items<double>(); }
double price1(const mod::Field &f) { return prices(f)[0]; }

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln("maturity   Original              LittleTrap   COS (reference)");
  for (double maturity : {0.25, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 10.0, 15.0}) {
    auto original = strip(mod::Engine::Fft, {100.0}, {maturity});
    original.controls.fft.mode = prc::HestonCfMode::Original;
    auto stable = strip(mod::Engine::Fft, {100.0}, {maturity});
    stable.controls.fft.mode = prc::HestonCfMode::LittleTrap;
    std::string shown;
    try {
      shown = std::to_string(price1(original));
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      shown = "refused";
    }
    out.writeln(maturity, shown, price1(stable), price1(strip(mod::Engine::Cos, {100.0}, {maturity})));
  }
}
```

The two forms agree through two years (12.4385 at two years), and the COS price equals both. Beyond that the original form is not just less accurate: the FFT engine refuses it at five years and longer rather than return a wrong price. `LittleTrap` equals the COS price at every maturity (20.5643 at five years, 29.3176 at ten and 35.1073 at fifteen).

## Maturity zero and invalid inputs

At zero maturity the price is the intrinsic value. A field checks its model before it prices, and reports every parameter outside its domain, naming it. The Feller condition is not one of them.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
namespace prc = fin::pricing;
using namespace pluss::tils;
using pluss::Series;

constexpr double kSpot = 100.0, kRate = 0.03, kDiv = 0.01;

// A strip of options on one underlying priced under Heston (or Bates, with eight parameters) by the chosen engine.
mod::Field strip(mod::Engine engine, const std::vector<double> &strikes, const std::vector<double> &maturities, std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04},
                 prc::OptionType type = prc::Call, fin::model::Kind kind = fin::model::Kind::Heston) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = {.type = type, .strike = pluss::make_series(strikes), .maturity = pluss::make_series(maturities)},
                    .market = fin::market::make(kSpot, kRate, kDiv),
                    .model = fin::model::make(kind, params),
                    .controls = {.engine = engine, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
}
std::vector<double> prices(const mod::Field &f) { return f.price().price.items<double>(); }
double price1(const mod::Field &f) { return prices(f)[0]; }

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  out.writeln("maturity 0: the call struck at 90 is worth", price1(strip(mod::Engine::Cos, {90.0, 100.0, 110.0}, {0.0})) );
  out.writeln("a variance process that violates the Feller condition is allowed (2 kappa theta =", 2 * 0.2 * 0.02, "< sigma^2 =", 0.8 * 0.8, "):",
              price1(strip(mod::Engine::Cos, {100.0}, {1.0}, {0.2, 0.02, 0.8, -0.7, 0.04})));

  auto attempt = [&](const char *label, std::vector<double> params) {
  try {
    (void)strip(mod::Engine::Cos, {100.0}, {1.0}, params).price();
    out.writeln(label, "-> no error");
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string message = e.what();
    out.writeln(label, "->", message.substr(0, message.find('\n')));
  }
  };
  attempt("kappa = -1  ", {-1.0, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04});
  attempt("theta = 0   ", {1.5, 0.0, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04});
  attempt("sigma = 0   ", {1.5, 0.04, 0.0, -0.7, 0.04});
  attempt("rho = 1     ", {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, 1.0, 0.04});
  attempt("v0 = -0.1   ", {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, -0.1});
  attempt("5 values for a 4-parameter list", {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7});
}
```

At zero maturity the call struck at 90 is worth its intrinsic value, 10 (the spot is 100). A variance process with 2κθ = 0.008, far below σ² = 0.64, prices without complaint. Each invalid parameter is rejected with a message that names it, and a parameter list of the wrong length is rejected too.

